From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 18:23:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: glm53f-afd's expert prompt kernels for GLM's
 prompt chunks, TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL=g53 (glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc; MiaAI-Lab
 recipe patches 0009, 0020 reimplemented)

Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden) authored these expert prompt kernels in glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc
(https://github.com/hughmadden/glm53f-afd, MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Turquoise Bay AI Pty Ltd): the
plan, the large-M gate/up and down kernels, and the fused epilogue and reduce of crates/glm53f-rank/kernels/
exl3_rank.cu, at the two-rank width (1,024 channels a rank) with its once-a-row rotation and its group order, run on
the AFD expert node's resident TP2 half for a prompt chunk's routed experts. Its EXL3 decoder, fragment MMA and
Hadamard butterfly come from TensorFold by Ash Hart (@ashxhart, github.com/ashhart; TensorFold @ bb4b4a3, MIT); its TP
split of every expert by intermediate channel follows glmrt by T.J. Purtell (@wrldsuksgo2mars, github.com/tpurtell);
its ROWROT (one rotation a row for a layer whose experts share one gate/up suh) and its group order were reimplemented
from MiaAI-Lab's GLM-5.3-Flash EXL3 TensorFold recipe @ 1576746, patches 0009-glm-prefill-kernels and
0020-glm-prompt-experts-order (Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2026 MiaAI-Lab), and the port chooses that rotation by
her recipe's shared_suh test (already in cuda/exl3/experts.py, ported unchanged).

- families/glm5_next/cuda/g53_rank.cu (new): glm53f-rank's kernels with data movement changed only. 17 units are
  byte-identical to glm53f-rank's (mcg2, decode_tile, mma16816, fwht128, the load helpers, down_tail, reduce_kernel,
  order_kernel, launch_persistent, ...); 15 are adapted: the weights are this node's layer (each expert's gate, up and
  down trellis through the layer's pointer tables, the stacked suh/svh vectors; no second copy of any weight) in place
  of glm53f-rank's layer image; the rows are the node's BF16 rows (any row stride; a BF16 value widened to FP32 where
  glm53f-rank widens an E4M3 code and multiplies its UE8M0 scale) in place of FP8 rows; the routes are the node's
  pick/wts rows (the 8 routed slots); the output is the half's FP32 rows (glm53f-rank's g53r_ffn_f32) in the node's
  reply buffer; no host staging or sync; an invalid route empties the plan as there and fills the call's rows with NaN.
  The split (decode-size) kernels, the rotation warps, the self-planning blocks and the dependent launch are not
  ported. g53_rank.h / g53_rank.cpp: the launch's arguments and the torch binding (every tensor checked).
- families/glm5_next/cuda/g53_experts.py (new): the switch, the layer scope (4-bit mcg, 288 experts, 4,096 -> 1,024),
  glm53f-rank's large-M default configuration (up to 2,048 rows: 32-row groups, one K split each; above: 64-row
  groups), the plan's buffers (the large ones are views of the node's prompt scratch), routed() and the node hook.
  The extension tensorfold_glm_g53_v1 is built with glm53f-rank's flags (-O3 --fmad=false --ftz=false
  --prec-div=true) and loaded when the node starts.
- families/glm5_next/cuda/experts_node.py: the expert node marks its MODE_CHUNK, MODE_SLICE and MODE_PAIR forwards as
  prompt chunks; ExpertNode reads the switch before the load and, when it is set, compute runs a prompt chunk's window
  of an in-scope layer through g53_experts. Decode windows (MODE_EAGER) never take it.

The arithmetic (glm53f-rank's): every row is rotated (x suh, the 128-point butterfly in FP32, x 1/sqrt(128), FP16);
gate and up are one FP32 MMA chain a sum over ascending k tiles (one K split; FP16 products); the fused epilogue (the
last of a group's blocks) sums the splits in order, rotates back, x 1/sqrt(128) x svh, GLM's clamped BF16 SwiGLU,
x down suh, rotates, FP16; down is one FP32 MMA chain a sum; the fused reduce (the block bringing a row's chunk to
its 8 arrivals) adds the 8 slots in slot order, acc + w * (rot(z) x 1/sqrt(128) x svh), each multiply and add
rounded on its own. A row's bits depend on its own values only. They are glm53f-rank's, not the grouped kernel's
(split-K partial sums, then combine), so a prompt row's routed-expert sum changes class when the switch is set.

TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL is unset by default (and "tf" is the same): every call is the one before, and no existing CUDA
source changed. "g53": prompt chunks of TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL_ROWS rows or more (default 65, glm53f-rank's large-M
boundary; up to 4,096) on in-scope layers run through these kernels; TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL_ROWROT (default 1, as
glm53f-afd serves it) and TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL_ORDER (default 0) choose the two schedules, which keep every bit. Any
other value refuses before the load. The new CUDA source needs a prebuild entry (tensorfold.families.glm5_next.cuda.
g53_experts._ext) wherever the extensions are prebuilt.

Tests: tests/test_glm_g53_kernel.py (CPU, 32: the documented arithmetic in tests/glm_g53_reference.py, op for op: its
butterfly is the Hadamard transform, in float64 the pipeline is the dense math of diag(suh) H W_q H diag(svh) with
GLM's clamped SwiGLU, with the kernels' roundings it stays within their rounding error, the row rotation gives the
per-matrix rotation's bits, the reduce's order; glm53f-rank's plan and group bound at 65-4,096 rows; its default
configuration; its two-rank image layout; the switch's names, defaults and refusals; unset, compute makes the calls
before; set, only prompt chunks of in-scope layers take the kernels; the node refuses a bad value before the load and
shares its prompt scratch). tests/cuda/test_glm_g53_kernel.py (GPU, on an expert node's GPU: against glm53f-rank's own
kernels built in the test from its pinned source, through its C API, on an image cut from the same tensors: the FP32
rows byte-equal at 65-4,096 rows, both group sizes, uniform and skewed routing, rotation once a row and per (row,
matrix), group order on and off, BF16 and FP32 SwiGLU, and the intermediates route by route; the same bytes twice and
a row's bits whatever its window; faults; the distance from the grouped kernel's outputs, bounded; both against the
CPU reference; the node's dispatch; times at 2,048 and 4,096 rows).

Credit: the kernel file's header, g53_experts.py, THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM expert prompt kernels from glm53f-afd
(TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL=g53)"), LICENSES/glm53f-afd-MIT.txt (glm53f-afd's LICENSE) and
LICENSES/glm53f-afd-LICENSE.tensorfold.txt (its LICENSE.tensorfold), both verbatim.

